      <div class="container "><div class="content-box content-box--large content-box--text-left content-box--left text-container"><h3 class="heading h2">The thing I never told anybody</h3><p>Here's what I was embarrassed to admit, even to my own family:</p><p>I'd started <em>planning around</em> my nose.</p><p>I'd think twice about a dinner with friends because I didn't want to be the one sniffling and clearing my throat all night. I slept propped up on two pillows because lying flat meant a post-nasal drip cough that kept me — and my husband — awake. I kept a quiet little arsenal in my purse: a decongestant, an antihistamine, a backup pack of tissues, the nasal spray I knew I was leaning on a little too hard.</p><p>From the outside, I looked fine. Inside my own head, I felt foggy, tired, and frankly a little ashamed of how much energy I was spending just trying to breathe like a normal person.</p></div>
